auxv6 is based on the work of MIT's xv6 project (x86 version)

We've created a more fleshed out version of xv6 with the goal
of supporting a custom classic MacOS UI- somewhere in the sweet
spot between System 7 and OS 9. Think of it as a jankier OS X
or a more modern A/UX (thus the name). 

At this point we have a lot of common drivers for network cards,
virtio devices, basic audio support, a custom X server, networking,
several custom 6utils (clones of popular software, i.e. 6vi, 6get,
6nano, etc), basic POSIX suppory, a LibreSSL port, and much more.

We're also lacking a lot of other features such as threading, 
true SSL/TLS integration, a better ports structure, more WMS, etc.

The MacOS UI is going to take a while to land, as our current focus
is landing all the little pieces we need to operate like a modern
Unix-like OS, but as we work towards that goal, it becomes easier
to put the UI in the driver seat. For now, we have dwm+st for those
wanting to start a graphical session. The system consumes 4MB when
running in a non-graphical session (via serial) and ~10 with the 
framebuffer active. 

We're planning for the WM to implement the top macos bar by implementing
custom headers that hijack the GNOME/KDE/<toolkit> menubar logic and
wiring it in so that our top menubar handles it instead. This is a 
lofty goal for certain, but with some targeted patches to GTK/qt this
isn't likely to be as impossible as it seems on the ground. Retooling
xv6 into a somewhat competent unix-like was pretty lofty, too, eh?

The elephant in the room: this is without a doubt, unequivacally, 
and entirely a: SLOPERATING SYSTEM. The vast majority of the code and 
all of the roadmaps, planning, and documentation are done by LLMs. 

I write a bit of code here or there, but there is absolutely no way
I can write the sheer quantity of C code required otherwise. 

If that turns you off, that's absolutely fair, no judgement. This is 
more of a project to keep me out of the bars, anyway, and it still
requires a substantial amount of editing/user direction to get what
is basically glorified autocorrect to generate the desired code anyway.

With that said, we're open for suggestions and PRs and whatever.

DISCLAIMER: DO NOT TRUST YOUR DATA TO OUR FILESYSTEM IMPLEMENTATIONS
THEY SHOULD BE MOSTLY CORRECT BUT I WOULD NOT RISK MY KIDS BABY PICS
TO ANYTHING WRITTEN BY AN LLM. NO WARRANTY, EXPRESS NOR IMPLIED APPLIES.
